http2: HEADERS priority from the request's priority header, like Chrome

Chrome gives a request of priority p (the u=p of its "priority" header) the
HTTP/2 weight round(1 + (7 - p) * 255 / 7), sets the exclusive bit and makes
the stream depend on the newest stream still open with priority p; only
without one on the newest open stream of the nearest higher priority, else
on 0 (Chromium's Http2PriorityDependencies::PriorityLowerBound()).
curl-impersonate sent weight 256 and dependency 0 for every request.

For profiles with Chrome's exclusive priorities, a request with a priority
header now gets that weight and dependency. A CURLOPT_STREAM_WEIGHT set by
the application keeps its weight: it is told apart from the profile's own
setting by a new stream_weight_set bit. Requests without the header keep
the profile's priority and count as priority 0 for later streams. PRIORITY
frames and HTTP/3 are unchanged.

diff --git a/lib/easy.c b/lib/easy.c
index 3277e1f..beb0bf5 100644
